{"timestamp": 1778697204.8181546, "duration_sec": 2.319042921066284, "validator_hotkey": "5GQyFzvtVMw9X45t6dvUFgR9unFpDCccPPCiAoqJrtPuC8my", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "mainnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "Yeah, it sounds really interesting, and I definitely like the idea of being able to run stuff from my machine. Now, is it designed to actually exercise the UI, or does it just do unit tests like stuff like some of these other systems do? I haven't really even looked into what it is. You know, I've only been, in the little bit of playing I've done, I've only been doing just bare-bones stuff."], [0, "unit tests with no UI. From what I believe, I've heard is that there's problems with trying to get UI automation running on it. A lot of the other things I think are having trouble getting hooked in properly. People are changing some of their code management"], [0, "to try and get it to work where, you know, if they're using CocoaPods or sub-modules, they're sort of more vendoring that stuff in so that it's out there and not causing issues. But as far as getting UI testing out there, I don't know. I haven't heard if anyone's using Calabash or Frank or any of those out there for that."], [0, "Yeah, my previous client did some work with just using the creating JavaScript scripts to run the UI automation stuff, and they were able to kick off kind of a smoke test with the check-ins and builds. So it is possible, and there are definitely quirks. It would just crash for no reason, break the build, and it was just a kind of simulator hanging or whatever, but it's possible. Okay, and that was through the bot stuff?"], [0, "Not with the bots. We kicked off UI automation just through Jenkins. Oh, right. Yeah, we should get Jonathan Penn to come on and talk about UI automation with the JavaScript stuff because it looked pretty cool when he came and presented it to the Salt Lake Cocoa Heads. But I think it would be an interesting discussion to have here and just see how we can automate some of that stuff. Yeah, I would definitely tune in for that one."], [0, "All right, well, are there any other aspects of CI or your workflow in general and how this fits in that you want to go over before we get into the picks? Not really. I mean, with the bot stuff, along with that with the new stuff is that XCTest framework is sort of a rebranded OC unit. So if you're using the official XUnit test framework in Xcode where you just create a new test target and a new test class and sort of go the orthodox Apple route with it, now there's a new"], [0, "prefix for things it's it's from what i can tell i'm not terribly different at this point but i'm hoping it's setting the stage for some some more nice stuff but it seems like you know i'm trying to use more of the xc unit stuff uh my current project because i tried using specta recently and i think i just had horrible timing because uh"], [0, "You know, it was with the new Xcode 5, and I don't think it's had as much time to get up to speed with the bot stuff and everything. So XCTest is the new official Apple way of writing unit tests, and it's not BDD style. It's regular XUnit style, but it's pretty easy to grasp. So if you're looking for where to start, it may not be the prettiest or most hit, but it should get the job done for you. Yeah, and if you've got the old scent test cases,"], [0, "They pretty much map over one-to-one at this point, like you were saying, so you don't have to change your logic too much. They'll actually run the conversion for you, kind of. Right. It'll mess up your project, but it'll convert the test for you. You just got to fix up your project afterwards if you run the conversion. Yeah. Just that Xcode will attempt to convert things for you and make sure you're synced up and have a clean source code repository before you attempt it. Awesome."], [0, "All right. Well, I'm just going to throw my weight behind setting up a CI machine. I've seen this pay off on a lot of teams in a lot of ways. I've set it up on at least two or three of the teams that I've worked on. And overall, it's really nice to have that one place where you can say, okay, it either works or it doesn't."]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["Coding"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["continuous integration", "workflow automation", "unit testing", "ui testing", "ui automation", "xcode", "xcode bots", "xctest", "jenkins", "ios development", "test frameworks", "source control", "build pipeline", "cocoapods", "git submodules", "behavior driven development", "smoke testing", "tool integration"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778697816.3756113, "duration_sec": 2.5104002952575684, "validator_hotkey": "5HNe4Yx7idMcgrsoPogLVko2AA8Pap33aNyXQhpwBxsS4H4Q", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "mainnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "I have quite a few questions over here, right? Maybe we should kind of wind back because maybe not everybody knows what is TUI, right? Even for me, if you just said, hey, you know what a TUI is, I had to think about it sometime, maybe not even come to my mind, even though I know what this thing is, you know, what exactly is TUI, T-U-I?"], [0, "So TUI is an acronym for Terminal User Interface. It's basically like a UI in your terminal. And how that works is that we use basic characters to draw something like a UI. And you can use your keyboard or mouse to click things or move around or navigate. So we use the terminal to present you a usable interface, I would say."], [0, "So Tui is basically that and Ratatouille is the framework or library that is making you able to build these user interfaces. One example of that is, so in terminal, you know, we don't have like UI widgets or we don't have like graphical things."], [0, "we have just the characters so we use like a pipe character to like draw borders for example or we use like ui looking characters to show you basically like some widgets and at the end of the day it's very much like a gui but everything happens in the terminal that's a pretty good explanation and people may be wondering you know i could just do text-based myself but it's kind of like like you said it's more than just showing data and accepting data like it does seem like an actual like gui"], [0, "kind of framework and what people would consider with buttons and all this kind of stuff. I mean, if you go to ratatouille.rs, that GIF or GIF you have in the middle really starts to show some of the features that it does have, right? Like you have this multicolored background and like, you know, doing that by hand is not going to be easy, but it seems like it's pretty easy with here. And it's like, you can do charts."], [0, "You have this kind of like menu section. It's kind of like, you know, having your own DOS, but even more powerful with the design work that you can probably do with it. That seems like a lot of work involved, right? How much of Red 2 right now is actually just stuff written from a long time ago with 2ERS?"], [0, "First of all, TUI is not a new concept. It's been around for decades. But even like in the 19s, we had these DOS UIs. One good example is these editors that we had, you know, with the blue background and stuff like that."], [0, "we were able to do these types of stuff for a long time. And in the Rust world, there were a couple of terminal manipulation libraries. One of them is cross-term, and it was just aimed to give you the full control of the terminal, meaning that it uses ANSI escape codes to draw something or like change the mode of the terminal and so on. And later on in 2ERS, what Florian did is that take that logic, take that backend,"], [0, "and build some widgets on top of that. So in Ratatouille, we support multiple backends. Like I said, we use cross-term to manipulate the library and we use some kind of like rendering mechanism. It's called immediate mode rendering. And we use that to render some widgets on top of it. So it basically works like that."], [0, "and like you said we have like numerous widgets we have list table charts and so on so all the heavy lifting is handled by some back end like cross term or like termion and so on and ratatouille is only responsible for drawing the actual ui on your terminal"]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["Coding"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["terminal user interface", "tui framework", "ratatouille library", "rust programming", "terminal graphics", "ansi escape codes", "immediate mode rendering", "terminal widgets", "keyboard navigation", "mouse support", "charts display", "backend libraries", "command line tools", "software engineering", "user experience", "interface design", "open source", "legacy computing"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
