{"timestamp": 1778708384.8027873, "duration_sec": 4.913416147232056, "validator_hotkey": "5HmkM6X1D3W3CuCSPuHhrbYyZNBy2aGAiZy9NczoJmtY25H7", "task_type": "named_entities_extraction", "task_raw": {"mode": "testnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "named_entities_extraction", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "document", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "Seattle Council Committee Report\nCouncil Briefing\nCouncil Chamber, City Hall\nMonday, April 27, 2026, 2:00 PM\n600 4th Avenue\nSeattle, WA 98104\nMeeting Start Time: 2:04 p.m.\nPresiding Officer: Council President Hollingsworth\nPresent: 7 - Joy Hollingsworth, Dionne Foster, Debora Juarez, Robert Kettle, Eddie Lin, Alexis\nMercedes Rinck,Rob Saka\nExcused: 2 - Maritza Rivera,Dan Strauss\n1. Approval of the Minutes\nInf 2818 Council Briefing Minutes (2026)\nThe April 13, 2026, Council Briefing Minutes were approved.\n2. President's Report\n3. Signing of Letters and Proclamations\n4. Preview of City Council Actions, Council and Regional Committees\nInf 2819 City Council Agenda (2026)\nThe Information Item (Inf) was heard in Committee.\nMeeting Adjournment Time: 2:55 p.m.\nPrepared by: Phillip Wood-Smith, Deputy City Clerk\nPage 1"]], "participants": null}}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "ner_001", "crc": 1321322, "title": "Extract named entities from documents", "name": "extract_named_entities", "description": "Identifies and extracts named entities from provided documents.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "ner", "prompt_template": "Extract all named entities from the provided document. Include the following entity types: PERSON, ORGANIZATION, LOCATION, and any other relevant entities. Return as comma-delimited list of entities with their types (e.g., 'John Smith (PERSON), ACM Corp (ORGANIZATION)). Only return the entities without any additional commentary.", "output_variable": "extracted_entities", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["joy hollingsworth", "seattle city council", "seattle city hall", "dionne foster", "debora juarez", "robert kettle", "eddie lin", "alexis mercedes rinck", "rob saka", "maritza rivera", "dan strauss", "phillip wood-smith", "city clerk", "seattle wa", "600 4th avenue", "inf 2818 council briefing minutes 2026", "inf 2819 city council agenda 2026"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778708597.2014723, "duration_sec": 1.7635879516601562, "validator_hotkey": "5HNe4Yx7idMcgrsoPogLVko2AA8Pap33aNyXQhpwBxsS4H4Q", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "mainnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "And this can actually happen, I think, and I saw this happen when you have these sub-crates or whatever we call it, right? You have like a workspace with many crates inside, right? Something like that? Yes, exactly. Exactly. Basically, this is a multi-repo, okay? So in the Rust world, it's called the workspace, but it's basically a multi-repo where you have several projects"], [0, "that are somewhat related in the same repository. And you have to put in the repository link in each one of the TOML files, in each one of the sub-creates. And it's a little bit strange for me because the URL is the same for each one of them, right? So I think the repository link should be able to be taken from the top TOML file where you list the sub-creates."], [0, "Because now it's easy to forget what I'm saying, that you add a new crate inside the workspace and then you forget, someone forgets to add the repository because it's not added by default. And that's how it might easily happen that even for a company that has several crates with the repository link and then some without."], [0, "Right? That can easily happen. And it's sort of a little bit boring adding this link to every one of these sub-crates when I notice that that happens. And I thought about it already, but I haven't done that. I should probably go and see if this could be added to the main terminal file and the root of the repository. And if not, then maybe to open an issue on cargo to allow this, because"], [0, "This is the same information for each one of the crates so probably there shouldn't be any reason to replicate it there"], [0, "Yeah, I can see how annoying that would be. And you wouldn't want to copy and paste that, right? Some kind of way that you can write it once. That'd make much more sense. Yeah, that's what I'm doing now. It's annoying. There must be a way. If not, yeah. I'm also not surprised if there's no way. This is the point where people start writing some wrappers around Gargo to copy this automatically because they don't have it in the central one. Or someone adds this, the extra possibility to add to the central Tomloss file."], [0, "Let me take a look. You have crates without owner, which is interesting. So you can have a crate without an owner. I don't even know how that even works. Is that possible? I don't know. Maybe it's a bug in RustDigger. Okay. So there is the crates without owner name, but the crates without owner, that's the most surprising, right? I don't know what's going on there. I think I didn't have time to really try to understand what's going on there."], [0, "I actually opened an issue on crates.io, I think somewhere, asking about the database and some of the fields that I didn't understand, because there is a database dump that I'm using, and I wasn't clear about all the fields. I think I haven't received a response yet, but, well, time will..."], [0, "do the thing. Now, one thing you might have noticed that in each of these lists in the reports, under the reports, these are actually ordered by the update at timestamp and the newest are the topmost. And well, right now there's only the 100 topmost ones are listed. I didn't add the paging, there's one of the issues that I opened, but sort of my"], [0, "understanding or the way I think is that if there is a project that hasn't been updated for two years, then it's quite unlikely that if you send a pull request for anything basically, that pull request will be accepted, right? But if a project was released just a year,"]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["Coding"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["rust", "cargo", "workspace", "crates", "multi repo", "toml files", "repository url", "metadata", "code duplication", "automation", "issue tracking", "crates io", "software tooling", "package registry", "database dump", "data fields", "report ordering", "pull requests"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778708685.993104, "duration_sec": 1.9149563312530518, "validator_hotkey": "5HNe4Yx7idMcgrsoPogLVko2AA8Pap33aNyXQhpwBxsS4H4Q", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "mainnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "nothing happened after a couple days or after some time then they started adding more stuff to it over time and eventually what happened was it wanted people to download it and as soon as you run it it's going to try to get information about your computer and then send it to a telegram bot but i mean this would also be interesting too is to see similarly named crates right and it would give an idea are they malware"], [0, "Or are they just names that are just similar? Like I think request is probably already taken. And then we have our famous request, which is spelled a little bit differently. If you can link those two together, it'd be interesting to see, is it malware or is it actually a real bonafide crate? That's a much bigger problem than, I think much more complex problems than the ones that I am addressing now."], [0, "Yeah, definitely. But it would just be interesting to see the similarly named crates, you know. I guess already if I can provide a list of all the crates that are, let's say, one letter distance from each crate. So a mapping of the crates of the distance and then, okay, it's sort of a warning. And then as a first step, someone can manually look at it. Okay, that's much better than trying to look at all that. How many hundred thousand crates that you have there?"], [0, "Yeah, I agree. That would be really cool. You were kind of already talking about a couple of things that you're working on right now and then things that you're thinking about adding in. Is there any immediate work that you're planning to put in like within the next couple of weeks, next couple of months that for sure is going to happen?"], [0, "I have some memory leak that I have to figure out. I'm just copying the list of all the crits several times and it blows up. So that I will have to fix soon. I'm working right now on the cloning of repositories. I used to have something, but now I need to improve it. And then I'm planning to run Clippy on them, on all the repositories, and then report that as well. So these are sort of the..."], [0, "the immediate things that I'm working on regarding this site. And yeah, I think these are the main things. I mean, the whole project is in GitHub and there are a few issues opened already. Mostly I opened them, but I would be really happy to have more people look at the site, tell me,"], [0, "I mean, even if they just open issues of what's not working properly or what kind of features they might think that could be useful on the website, that would be definitely giving me a boost of interest in implementing things. And obviously I would be happy to get people looking at my code. It's very far from being good Rust code at this point, but slowly it's going to improve."], [0, "Well, that was going to be my follow-up question is if you're looking for any help and where they can see that, but like you said, you have a GitHub, you have issues on there. Basically, people can start to contribute new issues, so basically new ideas, bugs, whatever, and also contribute actual code besides just contribute the issues themselves, right? Yeah."], [0, "Yeah, and of course, I mean, I would be really happy if people started to use the website in order to fix issues in all kind of... These are small issues, basically, right, at this point, in all kind of all of the crates. What I do now is sort of..."], [0, "sneaky marketing strategy of the website is that every time I send a pull request, once it's accepted, then I comment on the pull request linking to the webpage where all the creators of the person is listed so they can look at the website. And some people quite liked the idea and they thought that it will be useful for them. Right now the whole thing is a static website."]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["Coding"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["rust crates", "malware detection", "typosquatting", "crate names", "edit distance", "static website", "memory leak", "repository cloning", "clippy linting", "github issues", "pull requests", "open source contributions", "supply chain security", "software engineering", "code quality", "vulnerability research", "marketing strategy", "telegram bot"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
