 the Seattle City Council aiming to provide a..."]], "participants": null}}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "ner_001", "crc": 1321322, "title": "Extract named entities from documents", "name": "extract_named_entities", "description": "Identifies and extracts named entities from provided documents.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "ner", "prompt_template": "Extract all named entities from the provided document. Include the following entity types: PERSON, ORGANIZATION, LOCATION, and any other relevant entities. Return as comma-delimited list of entities with their types (e.g., 'John Smith (PERSON), ACM Corp (ORGANIZATION)). Only return the entities without any additional commentary.", "output_variable": "extracted_entities", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["seattle city council", "city of seattle", "seattle", "seattle city hall", "councilmember dan strauss", "councilmember joy hollingsworth", "councilmember robert kettle", "councilmember rob saka", "councilmember maritza rivera", "doug trumm", "finance native communities and tribal governments committee", "council rules and procedures", "inf 2887", "centennial accord", "cb 121201", "2026 fifa world cup", "seattlefwc26", "youtube", "digital nomads"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778712620.820428, "duration_sec": 2.4702932834625244, "validator_hotkey": "5HmkM6X1D3W3CuCSPuHhrbYyZNBy2aGAiZy9NczoJmtY25H7", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "testnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[3, "That's how close I am. That's not easy. It's very hard for me."], [1, "It's 5:00 AM in Algodones. The sky is still dark, not quite sunrise, and Alberto is shuffling around the house, getting ready for for work. The streets are mostly quiet at this hour, just the sound of other promoters and vendors setting up, setting the stage for Molar City. By 6:00 AM, he's at his post. The border opens and the Americans descend. They walk in the shadow of the 30-foot wall that divides them. Crossing the very threshold Alberto cannot."], [0, "99% Invisible was reported and produced by Latif Nadaan and edited by Emmett Fitzgerald and Delaney Hall. Mixed by Martin Gonzalez. Music by Swan Real. Voiceover and translation support by Laura Ubate. Fact-checking by Graham Hayshea. Special thanks this week to Gabby Martinez and Grisel Castaneda. Kathy Tu is our executive producer. Curt Kohlstedt is the digital director. The rest of the team includes Chris Berube, Jason DeLeon, Christopher Johnson, Vivian Lei, Joe Rosenberg, Kelly Prime, Jacob Medina Gleason, Talon and Rain Stradley, and me, Roman Mars. The 99% Invisible logo was created by Stefan Lawrence. We are part of the SiriusXM Podcast family, now headquartered 6 blocks north in the Pandora Building in beautiful uptown Oakland, California. You can find us on all the usual social media sites, as well as our new Discord server. There's a link to that, as well as every past episode of 99PI at 99PI.com. LifeLock, how can I help?"], [4, "The IRS said I filed my return, but I haven't."], [0, "1 in 4 taxpaying Americans has paid the price of identity fraud."], [1, "What do I do? My refund, though\u2014 I'm freaking out."], [0, "Don't worry, I can fix this. LifeLock fixes identity theft guaranteed and gets your money back with up to $3 million in coverage."], [1, "I'm so relieved."], [0, "No problem, I'll be with with you."], [1, "Every step of the way."]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["Society & Culture"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["border crossing", "mexico border town", "street vendors", "dental tourism", "early morning work", "border wall", "restricted movement", "podcast production", "audio editing", "voiceover translation", "identity theft", "tax fraud", "immigration", "travel industry", "small business", "advertising sponsorship", "consumer protection", "financial anxiety"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778712841.8387408, "duration_sec": 1.6473162174224854, "validator_hotkey": "5C8LULGoFybR7ybg4S8qQkaaowGXFFja4KmMoSqwhxuAsn33", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "testnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[2, "It was very hard, you know. We seen the police and comes there. He says\u2014and Mariana says\u2014your work is not good. You have to give the money back to the lady."], [1, "Police in Los Algodones exist to protect the industry and to make tourists feel safe. There's an old promotional video for Molar City still up on YouTube. It's from 12 years ago, and it's less than a minute long. In the clip, a police officer does a salsa twirl with an elderly white woman. The video is titled \"All Tourists Are Safe and Fun in Los Lagodones.\"."], [4, "There's parties, there's fun, there's bars, there's cheap food and drinks, there's dental care, there's a ton of pharmaceuticals. And the police are, are watching. If there's any sense of like any trouble brewing, police will be very much on it."], [1, "Police might help tourists feel safe, But for locals, especially low-wage workers like street vendors and promoters, the police ensure that their movements are more tightly controlled. Remember those famous welcome parties, the ones that Dr. Medina started in the '80s? A lot of locals aren't invited. In fact, they're explicitly told to stay away."], [4, "There's a lot of expectations around where people are allowed to be on those days. Like, you know, definitely do not show up at the party unless you're helping to work there. And so on a day like the welcome party, that involves sort of restricting the movement of other folks who live there and making sure that, you know, they're only seeing and experiencing what the sort of town elites want them to see and experience to ensure that the reputation of the tourist industry is upheld."], [1, "Despite all this effort towards safety, whenever violence does occur anywhere in the border region, it can have major ripple effects on medical tourism. I spoke to someone named David Viquist, a medical tourism researcher who conducted a bunch of surveys on Americans after an incident in 2023 when 4 American medical tourists were ambushed by a drug cartel and only 2 of them survived. David was curious about Americans' perceived ideas about safety in Mexico and how those ideas might impact their decision to engage in medical tourism there. On one of his surveys, he asked Americans, \"Would you feel safe going to Mexico?\" And then another question: \"Would you feel safe going to Canc\u00fan?\" David's research suggested that Americans perceive Mexico and Canc\u00fan as two separate places. Canc\u00fan is safe. Mexico is not. In a way, I think Los Algodones has achieved that Canc\u00fan status. Molar City can exist as a performance for American consumers, disembodied from the forces that created it. And about how many people do you think in town have the same kind of job that you do?"], [3, "Oh, my competition."], [1, "Does it feel like competition?"], [3, "Well, I got them all, you know, I got them all pointed out. I got a report on every single one of them. Which one would you want me to start with? I got\u2014 my competition's everywhere. Everybody's my competition."], [1, "There are dozens of street promoters like Alberto, and because there are so many of them, all working on commission, Alberto's success depends on his charisma. He has to give the best performance. And so Alberto has his own strategies to try to stand out and make a good impression. Besides workshopping comedic material on the job, he told me this one other thing. He tends to play up how American he is. It's not a hard thing for him to do because like many people who grew up along the border, Alberto traveled back and forth a lot. He lived in Arizona but crossed the border weekly. His English is good. He describes himself as being \"from the border.\"."]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["Society & Culture"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["police", "tourist safety", "medical tourism", "border town", "los algodones", "dental care", "pharmaceuticals", "street vendors", "promoters", "low wage workers", "movement control", "competition", "public relations", "hospitality industry", "drug cartel", "survey research", "consumer perception", "performance marketing"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778712919.9183056, "duration_sec": 3.1428699493408203, "validator_hotkey": "5C8LULGoFybR7ybg4S8qQkaaowGXFFja4KmMoSqwhxuAsn33", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "testnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[3, "Well, I got them all, you know, I got them all pointed out. I got a report on every single one of them. Which one would you want me to start with? I got\u2014 my competition's everywhere. Everybody's my competition."], [1, "There are dozens of street promoters like Alberto, and because there are so many of them, all working on commission, Alberto's success depends on his charisma. He has to give the best performance. And so Alberto has his own strategies to try to stand out and make a good impression. Besides workshopping comedic material on the job, he told me this one other thing. He tends to play up how American he is. It's not a hard thing for him to do because like many people who grew up along the border, Alberto traveled back and forth a lot. He lived in Arizona but crossed the border weekly. His English is good. He describes himself as being \"from the border.\"."], [3, "So I grew up, you know, going to school and then coming down the weekends down to Mexico. And a lot of kids grew up like that. Because there's a lot of people in Agua Dulce that were\u2014 oh, I forgot to mention I was deported."], [1, "Alberto was deported to Mexico in 2010. In fact, I learned that almost all of Molar City's street promoters are deportees. It is in fact their Americanness, their American English, that makes them ideal candidates for this role."], [3, "It helped me break the ice. It helps me bond with people. Right away they can tell that I lived in America."], [1, "Oh, they bring it up?"], [3, "Yeah, they'll say it like that. They'll be like, \"Oh, I can hear you. Oh yeah, you lived in the States, huh?\" I'm like, \"Yes. Got me a little agent McCasian over there.\" So it makes them\u2014 makes me relate to them more and feel more relaxed."], [1, "Alberto tells me he'll recognize familiar faces crossing into Mexico from time to time. So-and-so from high school, his friend's uncle. But he doesn't get to see his family so often. They all live in Arizona and don't like to come visit."], [3, "They really\u2014 they really only come when they have to."], [1, "Yeah."]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["Society & Culture"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["street promotion", "commission sales", "charisma", "competition", "performance", "border life", "american identity", "bilingual english", "mexico arizona", "deportation", "social bonding", "family separation", "immigration policy", "cross cultural communication", "informal economy", "personal branding", "border towns", "social networks"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778713073.040944, "duration_sec": 1.861532211303711, "validator_hotkey": "5HNe4Yx7idMcgrsoPogLVko2AA8Pap33aNyXQhpwBxsS4H4Q", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "mainnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[1, "Yeah. Yeah, I mean, it's amazing because the higher vibrational energies and the fact that we're multidimensional, we can reach into these higher dimensional energies, which include neutrality and just universal love. Not necessarily the fourth chakra love where you got to love everybody and you can love them sort of in a more observing, sort of neutral kind of way that actually dissolves black goo and AI and dark technology. And it's been actually seen to actually work. The black goo has been shown to be in its sort of death throes in the presence of super high vibrational energy, and it's called aurora plasma. And it had a similar\u2014 the black goo had a similar response as you would see to the burning of something with acid, right? It just completely dissolves. So we need to just be in a high vibration within ourselves. And sometimes that's not easy. And that's why being really strong and soul-centered. So if you're seeking answers, you got to understand that you have a lot of your own answers. And the more time you spend with nature, the more time you spend with things that help you to pull up to the surface a lot of the stuff that makes you vulnerable to confusion, that's all in the head, right? So it's important to learn as much as we possibly can. And we're all kind of seeking, right? We have all different purposes and missions and information to bring to the table. But I think the ones that are super defensive, that don't like to be questioned, that even the most humble and innocent of comments quest