{"timestamp": 1778714483.9248157, "duration_sec": 2.648902654647827, "validator_hotkey": "5HmkM6X1D3W3CuCSPuHhrbYyZNBy2aGAiZy9NczoJmtY25H7", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "testnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[2, "This was my first party. Look at American people, how they doing just in the street, you know? I don't have places to do it."], [1, "Medina flips through the book fondly, as if he's cooing at baby photos."], [2, "And that's the way Algodones start, you know, with that. And like this, you can see all my book is full of parties, you know, to where I go and everything, you know. Well, it's all my life in this book, you know."], [1, "It's clear that Medina put his heart into these parties, into making the town grow the way it did. He convinced his neighbors to cook enough food to feed thousands for free. He hired a mariachi band and converted an old boxing dressing ring into a stage. And the Americans had a great time. Medina made sure the margaritas were strong."], [2, "And they was drunk like crazy, you know. The American customs told me, Dr. Medina, why you give to those guys?"], [0, "Why?"], [2, "They cross right here, they don't want to show the papers, they tell us son of a bitch and go."], [3, "They was very drunk."], [1, "Year after year, Los Salgadones would continue to throw parties like that first one. Americans would return home, talk of the tacos, the town, their teeth. What was once a town of about 750 when Maga\u00f1a first arrived is now crammed with clinics, hotels, restaurants, and pharmacies. Today, young dentists and souvenir vendors from all over Mexico continue to migrate to town in hopes of finding stable work. And the tourists keep coming in droves."], [2, "In winter, it's too much people and they make lines, and that's uncomfortable for them, but they don't care. They make the lines, you know, and they still coming because they find dental services good, a good price and everything, and very handsome doctors. We live from the tourists, you know."]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["Society & Culture"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["dental tourism", "mexico town", "americans", "street parties", "community events", "free food", "mariachi music", "margaritas", "drunkenness", "border crossing", "customs officers", "local economy", "tourism growth", "dental services", "low prices", "clinics", "migration", "cultural exchange"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778714665.5475254, "duration_sec": 2.718785047531128, "validator_hotkey": "5DkzbwTnocecNohGP4aNVcHZfUgx8uA1MGQ4WB1u3kfi78gE", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "mainnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "That makes a lot of sense for first name and last name. That's pretty standard stuff, right? But what's cool about Isograph is that the client fields can return anything, including other React components. So the way Isograph has you think about building apps is as a tree of client fields. So you might imagine a homepage, which has a header, body, and a footer. And the header might select the avatar URL and whatever links you have available. Select some fields."], [0, "The way you would write that is by selecting the header, body and footer. And then in the header component, you would select the avatar component. And the avatar component, you would finally select a field that exists in the schema, the avatar URL. And then all of that gets compiled into a single query, which doesn't contain the header or the avatar component or anything like that."], [0, "but just contains the avatar URL. And if you select a client field in another client field, these are sort of composable in that sense, that client field, which is a set of data associated with a function, that function gets executed and the result is passed to the client field before the client field executes."], [0, "So you can sort of imagine that you write this tree of client fields, where it's client fields all the way down before bottoming out at server fields, and then it's all of the components that only use server fields are executed, and then you sort of go all the way up, up, up, up, up, back to the root, and then finally the homepage component is executed, and it gets a header."], [0, "which is the result of calling all these functions and the body and the footer, and then it does whatever it needs to do that. In particular, it just like, you know, paints it to the DOM or something."], [0, "Before we go off too much, I almost forgot that both of us at least know a bit about GraphQL. Maybe we can kind of talk a little bit about what is GraphQL and talk about the different types of, I don't know what you call those, because you have queries, mutations, and also subscriptions. Because I'm also kind of curious about if all of those work well with ISOGraph, but maybe we first talk about the basics of GraphQL and then we can talk about that part. Yeah, sure. So, GraphQL is a language for making network requests to a server."], [0, "So in particular, it has one killer feature, which is sort of fragment composability. So you might imagine that the header has a header fragment, and in that fragment, we include another fragment, which is the avatar component fragment. Maybe we can talk about what a fragment is because I'm entirely sure of you too. You're right, you're right, you're right. Yeah, keep me honest, keep me honest here. A fragment is just a collection of fields. So a field is something that the server knows how to provide. So a user's name or user's age or whatever are fields."], [0, "And GraphQL defines this tree of fields. So you might start at the root, which is usually the query object, and that might have a user with a given ID. And then on that user, you might be able to select the name or their best friend, which is another user and so on and so forth. And so you build up this entire giant like sort of tree. And then in order to execute, when you actually make a network request, you pick a finite set of fields from this"], [0, "possibly endless tree that the server defines. And then that server gives you back that tree in sort of a JSON format containing exactly the fields that you asked for and no more, no less. So the killer feature of, well, there's two killer features, I guess, of GraphQL. One is that you can select exactly the fields that a given screen needs. So in particular, it means that in a properly structured GraphQL app, the data that you get for a"], [0, "homepage is exactly the data that that homepage needs. And the second killer feature is fragment composability. So in a properly structured GraphQL app, each sort of component or file or function will define a fragment that includes the data that it specifically uses. So the avatar component fragment might select the avatar URL field. And then because that fragment is contained in another fragment,"]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["Coding"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["react components", "isograph", "client fields", "server fields", "component tree", "query compilation", "graphql", "network requests", "fragments", "fragment composability", "queries", "json responses", "data fetching", "web development", "user interface", "schema design", "application architecture", "modular composition"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
